  "notes": "The naming act, and the parameters that make it are orchestral rather than percussive. Three things separate this from every later trap entry: the hats are the *least* rolled on the axis — the research is explicit that rolls are section markers, not continuous — so `rolls.freqPerBar` is 0.12 against Gucci's and Migos's 0.20; the 808 barely glides (`slideProb` 0.15) because Toomp's sub is a sustained tuned note doubling the root with an octave drop once or twice per four bars, which is what `slidesPer4Bars` [1, 2] and the `P8`-weighted interval set say; and the melody is the only one here authored as `long_arc` with an arch contour, because the source material is replayed string swells and brass stabs rather than a two-bar bell cell. `secondaryAnchor` is `2a` — the syncopated 16th pickup *into* beat 3 the Micro-Chop breakdown describes, not a kick on 3 itself. ⚠ `chords.progressionFamilies` names the two families the research states (i–VI–VII, i–VII–VI) plus the static tonic it calls \"tonic-heavy, rarely modulates\"; the two-chord reductions `i–VI` and `i–VII` carry the lowest weights and are INFERRED — they exist because the generated chord part still owes a producer 55 distinct outputs over 200 seeds, and they are the first thing to delete if this ever sounds busier than the records. ⚠ `halfStepDissonance` 0.12 is the research's \"one chromatic upper voice\" read as a rate, which is an interpretation of a prose sentence rather than a measurement. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
